            <h2 id="bring">What does it bring to you?</h2>
            <p>Let's say you use Google or DuckDuckGo.
            What are your options when you don't like some website?
            You have there an option to block results from this domain but what if you don't want to full-on block it
            but rather decrease the results to show them lower in results page and see less of them?
            Or what if you like results from the domain and want to see them higher and more of them?</p>
            <br><p>Or, or!
            What if you'd like to apply whole filters and see only results from  trusted tech blogs,
            small websites or there are many more of these filters and anyone can create them!</p>

            <h2 id="use">How to use it?</h2>
            <p>Every time you make a search there is a "Quick Tune" (this is how it's called) icon at the right corner.
            If you wish to personalize results, either boost, reduce or block results from a domain, you click it and adjust results page.</p>
            <a href="/search?t=all&q=Apple" target="_blank"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Search Quick Tune example"></a>
        <p>➖ icon means you don't want to tinker with the domain, and you want to fully let PriEco decide how it gets ranked. (It's default behavior)</p>
        <p>👍 icon means you like results from this domain, and you want to see them higher in the results page and more of them.</p>
        <p>👎 icon means you don't like results from this domain and want to see them lower in the results page and less of them.</p>
        <p>🚫 icon means you hate results from this domain and don't want to see them at all.</p>
        <br><p>After you decide how you want the domains to perform, you need to perform the search again.</p>
        <img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Quick Tuned">
        <p>A new menu will appear in Quick Tune with all modified domains. So that you can modify the changes.</p>
        <br><p>At the top right you have a download button. It exports your changes as a Goggle.</p>

        <h2 id="goggles">Goggles: Sets of Rules!</h2>
        <p>As you've maybe noticed in Quick Tune, in the tutorial above, there is written "You have 0 active Goggles"
        and as I just said, you've exported your Quick Tune modifications as a Goggle.</p>
        <br><p><b>So, what is it?</b></p>
        <br><p>A Goggle is set of rules by which PriEco ranking has to behave.
        It's what you've already done in Quick Tune but on much larger scale.
        Or well, we have created a Goggle together already, so at this scale too.
        They work the same way as Quick Tune but are a bit more customizable and contain a bit more rules.</p>
        <br><p>The main reason for Goggle's existence is their shareability.
        PriEco has a whole <a href="/goggles" target="_blank">store</a> of Goggles you can choose from, and this is where the link in Quick Tune leads.</p>

        <h2 id="standard">Standard</h2>
        <p>There are a few other web search engines that support similar feature.
        I could have created my own too, and it would have been even easier to do.
        But, I was aware that there is already a potential standard.
        A filter potentially serving up to 100M monthly active users, with open source, sharable rules.
        I decided to adopt its way of doing it.</p>
        <br><p>Maybe you know that there exists a web search engine called Brave search.
Their Goggles are the ones I talked about.
I saw adopting them as a great thing to do as I'd help to standardize them and as we use the same syntax, they are fully cross compatible between us.
This allowed PriEco users to use Goggles created for Brave search.</p>

        <h2 id="improvements">Improvements</h2>
        <p>I never want to just a copy-paste a feature or idea to PriEco without improving it and bringing own ideas to PriEco.
        That's not to say Goggles were copy-pasted.
        PriEco works differently than Brave search which meant that they had to be created from scratch.
        I just followed their rules' syntax.
        My main pain point with Brave search Goggles is that they have to created manually.
        Which means manually writing down to a file hundreds, potentially thousands of rules.
        This in my view dramatically slowed down their adoption as it's hard to create new ones.
        Even when Brave search is a popular search engine with billions months queries, there is only a handful of Goggles made.</p>
        <br><p>In PriEco you can just export your configuration as a Goggle and enable it even in Brave search!
        This could allow creation of so many Goggles.</p>

        <h2 id="api">API</h2>
        <p>PriEco has an API so that companies and users get access to PriEco results for their own products and tools.</p>
        <br><p>Once a Goggle is in PriEco store, API customers can specify its ID in API requests, thus customizing their product experience too.
        This results in higher usage of Goggles.</p>

        <h2 id="hosting">Hosting</h2>
        <p>Brave search requires Goggles to be hosted on GitHub or GitLab.
        As it's just a file online I wanted to allow hosting them anywhere.
        You can use the mentioned platforms but even any other publicly accessible place on the internet.
        It must open up in pure form without any styling tags, just the Goggle data itself.
        Exactly what you get from PriEco export, for example.</p>

